Experiment-(b): Use sed command to swap the first and second words of each line in a file.

Command: sed -i 's/^$[^ ]*$$.*$ $/ \2 \1/' name.txt

Explanation:
i) sed : stream editor used to modify text.
ii) -i - modifies the file directly.
iii) ^ - indicates the beginning of a line.
iv) $[^ ]*$ - matches and stores the first word.
v) $.*$ - matches and stores the remaining text.
vi) \2 \1 - reverses the order, putting the second part first.
vii) name.txt - input text file.

Procedure:
1. Open the terminal.
2. Now, create a file named name.txt using the cat command and enter the names.
3. Press Ctrl + D to save the file and return to the terminal.
4. Use the following sed command to interchange the first and last names:
sed -i 's/^$[^ ]*$$.*$ $/ \2 \1/' name.txt
5. Display the modified file using cat name.txt.
6. Observe that the first and last names have been interchanged.
Result: The first and last names were successfully interchanged using the sed command.
